# Line endings and file types.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# The Store repo already has a scar from this: MEMORY.md §4 records that
# "PowerShell Set-Content -Encoding utf8 writes a BOM that makes JSON.parse fail
# on an invisible character". Git silently converting LF to CRLF in the working
# tree, and CRLF back to LF in the repository, produces the same class of
# problem — a diff that shows every line as changed, and a file whose bytes
# differ between machines.
#
# So: LF in the repository and in the working tree, on every platform, for
# every text file. CRLF is a Windows-only convention that git should not be
# making decisions about on your behalf.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly LF, in case a future editor disagrees with the rule above.
*.dart   text eol=lf
*.rs     text eol=lf
*.kt     text eol=lf
*.kts    text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.xml    text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.lock   text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf

# Binary. Never let git try to normalise these, and never let a merge try to
# resolve them as text — a conflicted APK is worse than a rejected merge.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.bin    binary
*.wasm   binary
*.so     binary
*.aar    binary
*.apk    binary

# GitHub language statistics ignore vendored and generated trees, so the repo
# does not report the app as "mostly vendored Rust" or "mostly generated Dart".
rust/src/engine/** linguist-vendored
lib/src/api/**      linguist-generated
rust/src/frb_generated.rs linguist-generated